214
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:03,980
I thought I was going to ask a tough question.

215
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:05,580
This is great.

216
00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:06,260
What the hell?

217
00:19:06,440 --> 00:19:07,160
Let's do it.

218
00:19:10,540 --> 00:19:11,880
There's a lot to that.

219
00:19:12,040 --> 00:19:13,400
There's a lot to unpack there.

220
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:15,400
I don't want to steal all the thunder from Aaron,

221
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:17,880
so I'll leave at least half the question I have here.

222
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:27,140
It starts out with a system that truly benefits from keeping us divided.

223
00:19:27,860 --> 00:19:31,500
It truly benefits from keeping us apart from one another.

224
00:19:32,540 --> 00:19:39,180
When I'm out knocking doors, and I've knocked a lot of doors, think of the most divisive issues that are used to rip us apart.

225
00:19:39,920 --> 00:19:42,540
They don't come up on the front doorstep.

226
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:44,500
They do not come up.

227
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:46,720
Affordability.

228
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:52,180
Fraud comes up some, but not as much as the headlines would have you believe when you're at the doorstep.

229
00:19:52,420 --> 00:19:53,840
They're worried about health care.

230
00:19:53,960 --> 00:19:55,200
They're worried about child care.

231
00:19:55,440 --> 00:19:59,780
They're worried about a lot of things that really impact them there at home.

232
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,780
And those are things we should be able to work together on.

233
00:20:03,880 --> 00:20:05,640
There's a lot of agreement on those things.

234
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:12,280
But as long as you can use a few wedge issues to keep everybody ripped apart,

235
00:20:12,480 --> 00:20:14,720
then you're going to continue to be successful.

236
00:20:14,940 --> 00:20:22,780
As long as you can use zero-sum politics, good guy, bad guy, enemy, whatever you call it.

237
00:20:22,820 --> 00:20:24,500
I'm not even comfortable with an opponent.

238
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:28,680
We should be running for the same thing.

239
00:20:29,780 --> 00:20:36,240
same as an opponent. We should both be trying to get the same job. And it's so disappointing.

240
00:20:36,380 --> 00:20:41,180
But there is a lot of interest in making sure that things don't work and we stay divided.

241
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:48,220
There are a lot of ways to exploit a dysfunctional system and not make drastic improvements.

242
00:20:49,360 --> 00:20:56,600
And that is a vested interest where you see money in politics actually invested. I've

243
00:20:56,600 --> 00:21:02,380
I've seen through your company where the reports where certain people,

244
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:07,660
it doesn't matter what side, you're donating to politicians on both sides, right?

245
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:11,660
That's not because they're trying to get something done.

246
00:21:11,700 --> 00:21:16,320
That's because they want influence, and no matter who's in power, they want to try this way.

247
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:20,060
But it's not ideological. It's not trying to move the ball forward.

248
00:21:20,200 --> 00:21:21,940
It's trying to bring the money back.

249
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:26,760
It's trying to accomplish a goal that may not be for the best of the people in Minnesota.

250
00:21:27,140 --> 00:21:37,480
So, there's a lot of answers to that, but I'm going to go with the vested interest in keeping us divided, using a few wedge issues as my answer.

251
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:47,280
Yeah, as Terran touched on, the division is what fundraises for both major parties.

252
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:54,680
You know, I look at the Republican Party, and they've been fundraising for years based off of that.

253
00:21:54,960 --> 00:22:04,020
If we could just get a little more money, a few more donations, we could take over the House or the Senate or the Governor's Office.

254
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:10,380
It's just, the division, it has to stop.

255
00:22:10,380 --> 00:22:17,800
It's used to keep us fighting over things that I'm not going to say don't matter because they absolutely do matter.

256
00:22:19,360 --> 00:22:23,620
But again, it's not serving we the people.

257
00:22:25,320 --> 00:22:34,960
The independent expenditures, the fact that certain organizations can spend unlimited amounts of money on a campaign has to stop.

258
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:38,580
Citizens United getting overturned are huge.

259
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:46,660
individuals have contribution limits. These PACs, they don't. They can fund whoever they want,

260
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:53,000
and they can pump as much money as they want in. And with that, I mean, we saw the

261
00:22:53,000 --> 00:23:01,180
derived result of that in Senate District 13. It just, the powers that be, as Trent said,

262
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:07,280
want to keep us divided. If they are able to funnel money through these PACs to support

263
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:13,500
their chosen candidates. The thing is, as Trent also stated, these same groups are funding

264
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:20,880
folks on both sides. It is meant for them to maintain control. All of us little guys cannot

265
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:27,740
compete with that, and they know it. So until we put a stop to that, I really don't see

266
00:23:27,740 --> 00:23:34,260
how things are going to change. Thank you. As a former mayor, I know that your personal

267
00:23:34,260 --> 00:23:38,840
life can be scrutinized and there are things in your past and things where you made mistakes

268
00:23:38,840 --> 00:23:44,060
that people can bring up. Is there anything about your past in your personal life that you would

269
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:57,520
like to address now? Yeah, so the justification for a number of folks to have someone run against

270
00:23:57,520 --> 00:24:06,720
me was based off of an arrest that happened. I have full custody of my two oldest kids.

271
00:24:07,360 --> 00:24:15,980
My ex had made some claims. I got arrested. I ended up fighting the charges.

272
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:23,680
I did tell the sheriff that I'd done better, so I agreed to plead guilty to disorderly conduct.

273
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:28,660
Less than a year and a half later, I was awarded full custody of my two oldest kids.

274
00:24:29,360 --> 00:24:35,500
I was trying court essentially twice and ultimately got custody of my kids.

275
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:40,280
I never went to try to get it expunged because it never affected my employment, my life, whatever.

276
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:42,360
And it's something that happened.

277
00:24:42,760 --> 00:24:50,080
You know, going through that, I guess, I'm not sure what future questions there are, but we absolutely need criminal justice reform.

278
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:56,280
I'm going to nod straight too far here, though, as well as some family court reform.

279
00:24:56,940 --> 00:25:04,320
But it's just, it's a shame that that kind of stuff was webinized.

280
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:08,640
I refused to speak ill of my ex, ultimately.

281
00:25:09,160 --> 00:25:11,000
She's biological on my kids.

282
00:25:12,760 --> 00:25:14,740
I didn't do it then.

283
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:16,720
I didn't do it after court.

284
00:25:17,500 --> 00:25:20,180
I'm not about to do it when I'm running for office.

285
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:22,800
The fact that that was weaponized is disgusting,

286
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:25,820
and the folks that did should be ashamed of themselves.

287
00:25:26,760 --> 00:25:30,880
When I spoke with certain folks in GOP leadership after this came up,

288
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,220
I said, listen, guys, I was a single dad in the state of Minnesota

289
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:37,360
that got full custody of my two kids.

290
00:25:37,640 --> 00:25:40,140
I wasn't married when the kids were born.

291
00:25:40,380 --> 00:25:43,380
That is a very, very uphill battle.

292
00:25:43,380 --> 00:25:47,320
courts do not just award custody to the father.

293
00:25:49,180 --> 00:25:51,640
And I even pitched to them,

294
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:53,480
we should be fundraising off of this.

295
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:58,680
But instead, some folks decided to amplify it,

296
00:25:59,340 --> 00:26:01,080
run to the media with it,

297
00:26:01,220 --> 00:26:02,720
and essentially just try to completely

298
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:03,540
disrupt my character.

299
00:26:04,060 --> 00:26:08,360
I had never given the fact that an arrest happened.

300
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:17,260
In fact, so I got full custody in August of 2011, and early 2012 is when I got involved in politics.

301
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:26,080
I had approached my state senator at the time, and I wanted them to work on changing custody laws based on what I went through.

302
00:26:26,360 --> 00:26:35,520
I knew if there was anything I could do to help the next person and family this might affect, I had an obligation to do so.

303
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:39,840
So the fact that this was weaponized against me, it was pretty disgusting.

304
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:49,180
I was going to tell the plot to, you know what you did last summer?

305
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:54,980
Then I remembered that things could be edited and I wasn't actually the convertible that hit a long short.

306
00:26:56,140 --> 00:27:00,240
No, I really don't have anything. They call me St. Trent.

307
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:10,680
So, no. I was lucky enough that my wild days in my 20s all stayed off of the record, so I'll leave them that way.

308
00:27:11,040 --> 00:27:16,580
Thank you. The next couple questions then direct towards just one of you and then I'll go to the next person.

309
00:27:17,080 --> 00:27:22,120
Let's start with you Trent. How did your military service shape your approach to politics?

310
00:27:22,440 --> 00:27:28,740
And what did you see that influences how you look at politics and the military?

311
00:27:32,020 --> 00:27:45,300
It changed a lot of it. It really did. It's very interesting to have a part of your life. I made the decision enlisted when I was 17 years old with a parent signature.

312
00:27:46,800 --> 00:27:56,960
And then a couple of short years later, well actually I think a year later, this place from the 90s, Iraq, opted to lose.

313
00:27:58,500 --> 00:28:10,520
And the next thing you know, my freshman year of college, I am called up and for the next two years I will live Iraq in ways that changed my life.

314
00:28:10,520 --> 00:28:17,440
Some for the better, some for the worse, but all because of a decision I made at 17.

315
00:28:19,060 --> 00:28:32,660
And by decisions made by people in Washington and politicians, some spineless, some self-motivated,

316
00:28:32,660 --> 00:28:37,420
None of that mattered when I was serving. It was all about the person next to you.

317
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:42,740
And nothing about the person next to you mattered.

318
00:28:42,980 --> 00:28:46,660
Your political belief, your religious belief, your ethnic background.

319
00:28:47,340 --> 00:28:51,400
None of that mattered because you were in it together.

320
00:28:52,320 --> 00:28:55,540
And I think we need more of that.

321
00:28:55,600 --> 00:29:02,420
That's an experience to know that service, on service with those you're with, does not matter.

322
00:29:02,660 --> 00:29:09,120
All the things we use to divide each other mean absolutely nothing when you're in a situation like that.

323
00:29:10,300 --> 00:29:19,080
As far as my political views and how I view the world, I am so, you know, I was a gravel road kid.

324
00:29:20,040 --> 00:29:24,080
I grew up, and my nearest neighbors were cornfields and then hog farms.

325
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:32,320
I worked on hog farms, worked in a meat packing plant to start to go to college before the GI Bill kicked in.

326
00:29:33,080 --> 00:29:42,580
And going to another part of the world, experiencing Iraq, the Iraqi people, seeing their lives,

327
00:29:43,480 --> 00:29:50,400
really was an expansion of my understanding and my concept of what's out there.

328
00:29:51,560 --> 00:29:56,180
Also coming back to the United States after seeing people with silver.

329
00:29:57,120 --> 00:29:59,140
The access of our country.

330
00:29:59,400 --> 00:30:04,260
And it was a very hard adjustment to make for a while,

331
00:30:04,420 --> 00:30:08,600
because all of a sudden there's everything everywhere.

332
00:30:08,900 --> 00:30:12,320
Everybody wants it now, and this latest inconvenience is the end of the world.

333
00:30:12,620 --> 00:30:15,460
And you just kind of, for a while, you want to shake people.

334
00:30:15,860 --> 00:30:18,460
And then later on it just becomes an alliance.

335
00:30:21,400 --> 00:30:28,100
those things and changed my view and took the groundwork and made me into a world be better.

336
00:30:30,200 --> 00:30:36,460
As we've seen some questions for you, Aaron, as we've seen this year, the value of the endorsement

337
00:30:36,460 --> 00:30:43,120
from the BPU use has significantly declined based on both sides of the aisle. We saw a lot of people

338
00:30:43,120 --> 00:30:49,980
who didn't get the endorsement and then go on to win the primary. In your experience as a BPU

339
00:30:51,400 --> 00:31:01,660
What do you think about going into it and then how has that shaped your view of politics and do you think we should continue on with the endorsing process in Minnesota or move to a primary?

340
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:11,340
So I've been involved for a while and historically in Senate District 13 we haven't had anything like this happen on the Republican side.

341
00:31:11,340 --> 00:31:21,320
At the state level, there's always been some disagreement, but ultimately the party has backed the endorsed candidates.

342
00:31:23,480 --> 00:31:27,660
For one reason or another, this election cycle, it did not go that way.

343
00:31:27,980 --> 00:31:34,600
I had the full support of the local Senate District 13 Republicans. They were absolutely great.

344
00:31:36,380 --> 00:31:44,360
They're in kind of a tough position right now. But throughout the process, they have supported me 100%.

345
00:31:46,160 --> 00:31:54,900
In terms of moving forward, I think ideally, you know, with the chaos that happened at the state convention,

346
00:31:54,900 --> 00:32:06,460
I think at the local levels, I feel more like House and Senate, we should still do endorsement conventions.

347
00:32:07,180 --> 00:32:16,860
Because otherwise, I guess I don't want to speak for Trent, but I highly doubt a person like Trent or myself would ever even be considered.

348
00:32:16,860 --> 00:32:24,020
because at the small levels, it would become whoever has the most money and can buy their way in, advertise their way in.

349
00:32:25,520 --> 00:32:29,360
The grassroots efforts matter. They truly do.

350
00:32:29,620 --> 00:32:32,520
I would love to say at the state level that they matter.

351
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:40,260
It seems they used to, but with all of the big money involved and how things are going,

352
00:32:40,260 --> 00:32:49,360
I think we would be better served if we would move at the state level to an earlier primary and then go from there.

353
00:32:50,020 --> 00:33:01,100
But again, at the local level, I would not change a thing. Otherwise, your average person who feels compelled to run to represent their district would never have a shot.

354
00:33:01,100 --> 00:33:03,680
Are you trying to agree with this?

355
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:14,800
Yeah, and I will point on the DFL side, I 100% of the endorsed carry the primary, so they did that 1,000.

356
00:33:15,400 --> 00:33:23,520
Local endorsement is important, and the process of building that.

357
00:33:23,520 --> 00:33:29,080
You know, we all have to do better of making sure more people come in.

358
00:33:29,740 --> 00:33:37,300
But I was just talking to somebody at a town hall, and they were railing against the fact that, you know, we're the young people.

359
00:33:37,800 --> 00:33:40,760
And I was tired. I was grumpy.

360
00:33:41,060 --> 00:33:43,440
So I answered very directly.

361
00:33:43,960 --> 00:33:47,300
A lot of this becomes a luxury, right?

362
00:33:47,960 --> 00:33:49,960
Participation in this process.

363
00:33:49,960 --> 00:34:06,260
When you're worried about making your mortgage payment, when you're worried about health care, when you're worried about all sorts of things, when you're being squeezed from every single direction, it becomes a bit of a luxury to be able to give your time and resources to the process.

364
00:34:07,260 --> 00:34:13,720
We have a lot of people who would love to get their voices heard, but they're just trying to make it to tomorrow.

365
00:34:14,520 --> 00:34:20,380
And so if we can find a way to try to bring that man earlier in the process, we're all going to be better for it.

366
00:34:20,560 --> 00:34:26,380
But I don't hold it against those who can't show up because stuff is hard. Life is hard right now.

367
00:34:26,680 --> 00:34:28,780
And they're going in a lot of directions.

368
00:34:30,260 --> 00:34:46,500
Paid Family Leave. Do you have an opinion on it prior to being implemented? Do you have an opinion on it now? And what do you think needs to change or what should have been done differently during the creation of it and the implementation of it?

369
00:34:51,420 --> 00:34:59,480
Pay Family Leave. I am supportive of Pay Family Leave. It has been done. It can be done right.

370
00:35:00,260 --> 00:35:10,680
I am critical of our current environment and how things are done. I was talking with a large, he's the CFO of a large business here,

371
00:35:10,680 --> 00:35:16,600
and we were really talking about were the businesses in the room, right?

372
00:35:16,800 --> 00:35:18,540
Were they in the room for the process?

373
00:35:18,920 --> 00:35:22,580
And there are a few things in it that made me think maybe they weren't in the room enough.

374
00:35:23,600 --> 00:35:27,520
Also, there has to be a way for the employer to provide invoice.

375
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:30,080
Right now, they just get the notification.

376
00:35:30,700 --> 00:35:32,860
So they don't have a voice in the process.

377
00:35:32,860 --> 00:35:37,240
They don't feel like they can provide any feedback, and that needs to be added to it.

378
00:35:37,240 --> 00:35:52,800
There's also a provision where I was just talking with a business owner in Paynesville, and where it covers, like they had a person who was working 10 hours on average for fund money.

379
00:35:53,740 --> 00:35:59,760
and that was clearly what they was not to pay the rent, it was not for health care, it was not to make ends meet.

380
00:35:59,980 --> 00:36:02,980
This literally was their job so they could go shopping.

381
00:36:03,700 --> 00:36:08,600
Ben found out that at 10 hours a week they were eligible for paid family leave.

382
00:36:08,740 --> 00:36:16,240
So now the small business owner who was trying to make it and barely making it has to pay that out as well.

383
00:36:16,740 --> 00:36:19,620
That was not the, I can't believe that was the intention.

384
00:36:19,620 --> 00:36:25,320
Now you have to be very careful about when you set the threshold, right?

385
00:36:25,580 --> 00:36:31,640
So if you make it 36 hours, Walmart will cap everybody at 35 hours.

386
00:36:32,540 --> 00:36:43,320
So you do have to set a threshold high enough that it makes sure that it would be a real pain to screw every worker who needs it the most out of it.

387
00:36:43,320 --> 00:36:49,180
But it was not intended for the 10 hour a week so I could go shopping employee.

388
00:36:49,440 --> 00:36:54,360
More voices in the room, more business owners in the room who have employees like that.

389
00:36:54,440 --> 00:36:57,580
You know, we see it in service industries, stuff like that.

390
00:36:58,240 --> 00:36:59,920
I think would have led to that.

391
00:37:00,140 --> 00:37:02,640
So the process needs to be more inclusive.

392
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:07,180
Part of that is the other side, whether it's my side, their side, whatever.

393
00:37:07,460 --> 00:37:12,720
The other side in general has to stop this pouting, take your ball, and go home crap.

394
00:37:13,740 --> 00:37:31,440
That is not how good legislation happens. If this is going to be developed and you have the experience and the knowledge to help it be better, it should be your duty in service to try to contribute that. You might not end up voting for the final thing, but you might be able to help improve the process.

395
00:37:31,440 --> 00:37:59,660
So that's it as well. I'm supportive of family leave. I believe we need to look at it and revise who it's impacting, how it's impacting, maybe the reach of it. But you shouldn't be one illness away from losing your job, losing your house, losing everything you have. You shouldn't be a family member taking intensive cancer treatments away from losing everything you have. This is the kind of safety net that is appropriate to make sure that we take care of people.

396
00:38:02,120 --> 00:38:08,180
I am opposed to paid family leave. I was prior to it being implemented and I definitely am now.

397
00:38:09,520 --> 00:38:16,540
It puts an unfair burden on small businesses. The company I work for, we have roughly 25 employees.

398
00:38:17,040 --> 00:38:23,400
If 10 of us decide to take that leave right now, my employer has to maintain our positions.

399
00:38:24,760 --> 00:38:27,520
but how is that going to work?

400
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:30,980
You know, it's not just the company of 25.

401
00:38:31,360 --> 00:38:35,900
There are plenty of small businesses of two people, five people, ten people.

402
00:38:37,760 --> 00:38:39,040
It is...

403
00:38:39,680 --> 00:38:43,040
People, we need accountability in general.

404
00:38:43,380 --> 00:38:46,360
We need personal accountability and our government needs accountability.

405
00:38:48,280 --> 00:38:55,860
You know, paid leave is something that it used to be a perk of certain employers.

406
00:38:56,220 --> 00:39:03,760
We are at low employment state. You get a job for financial gain and there are also other perks involved.

407
00:39:04,220 --> 00:39:09,700
Some companies offer paid leave. You know, when my kids were born, they offered paternity leave.

408
00:39:09,700 --> 00:39:16,200
I was able to take that, but I got that job knowing that and knowing that I wanted to start a family.

409
00:39:17,800 --> 00:39:25,700
Having the government implement that and put that burden upon small businesses, it's not right.

410
00:39:26,680 --> 00:39:34,240
I understand that, and I agree with Trent, people shouldn't be one illness away from losing everything.

411
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:37,340
However, we have other things in place to maintain your job.

412
00:39:37,340 --> 00:39:47,460
FNLA has been around for a long time. They will hold your job for you. It just doesn't put the same level of burden on the small company.

413
00:39:48,080 --> 00:39:55,320
And the other thing is, with how many people are taking it, with how it was implemented, they're going to have to increase the withholding.

414
00:39:55,480 --> 00:40:02,120
The employers are going to have to pay more, and all the workers are going to have to start paying more to pay for it as well.

415
00:40:02,120 --> 00:40:10,280
I just, I get the concept of it, I disagree with it fundamentally, and I think how it was rolled out was even worse.

